  "history": "Sugar has travelled further than most English words. It starts in ancient India, where Sanskrit śarkarā meant ‘grit, small stones’ and also sugar in grains. Sugar cane was grown in India, and the word moved west with the trade: Persian shakar, Arabic sukkar, medieval Latin succarum, Old French sucre. English took it from French around 1300. For a long time sugar was a luxury in England, used like a spice and kept locked away. Spanish azúcar still has the Arabic article al- ‘the’ at the front. And the Greek form of the same word, sákkharon, gave English saccharin, a sweetener made in a laboratory.",
  "historyEinfach": "Sugar is a very old word. It comes from India. In the old Indian language Sanskrit, the word meant ‘small stones’ and ‘sugar’. Then the word went to Persia, to the Arabs, and to France. English took it from French about 700 years ago. Sugar was very expensive then. Only rich people had it.",
